From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenBook <noreply@anybook.invalid>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] OPENBOOK: computer control: the device's own allowlists as a
 fallback

ComputerControlAllowlistController takes its session owner allowlist and
its automatable app allow and deny lists from DeviceConfig
(computer_control/allowed_session_owner_apps, allowed_automatable_apps,
blocked_automatable_apps), and persists the last valid value under
/data/system/computercontrol/. A device that gets no server configuration
has neither, so no agent can ever own a session.

Read a third source when both are empty: the device's own list in
/system_ext/etc/computercontrol/, under the same file names as the
persisted ones. Same syntax as the DeviceConfig value; entries may also
be separated by newlines, whitespace is ignored and lines starting with
'#' are comments. The factory value is never persisted, so DeviceConfig
still wins as soon as it has a value, and a persisted value still wins
over it.

Nothing else changes: the caller still needs ACCESS_COMPUTER_CONTROL,
POST_NOTIFICATIONS and the assistant role, entries are still pinned to a
signing certificate (or PREINSTALLED for automatable apps), and consent,
the lock screen and secure windows are checked as before.

OpenBook uses it to allow its privileged assistant, Quire, and the apps
it may automate (ANAL-216).
